# tools/crypto_chacha20_witness.rish — ChaCha20 (IETF, RFC 8439) authored in pure Rye: Season G's third crypto primitive, Monocypher's
# stream cipher, proven against the RFC 8439 known-answers and byte-for-byte against an independent implementation (Season G · Cryptography).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season G thread (Cryptography · Rye-native, Monocypher-parity, audit-ready).
# Design read: active-designing/20260815-180728_open-season-g-crypto-blake2b.md (the season's opening read names the ChaCha/Poly line as
# a primitive that follows the hash foundation, each its own round).
# Kin: crypto/blake2b.rye · crypto/sha512.rye (the first two primitives, GREEN). Monocypher (vendor/monocypher, CC0/BSD-dual) is the
# parity TARGET, never a copied line — our own arithmetic, studied clean-room from the public RFC (.claude/rules/gratitude-licenses.md).
#
# crypto/chacha20.rye authors ChaCha20 from the RFC — the four "expand 32-byte k" constants, the quarter-round, the twenty-round (ten
# double-round) block function, the little-endian keystream serialization, the counter-per-block stream XOR. The primitive calls no
# std.crypto. Parity is proven at selftest against two references runnable now: the RFC 8439 known-answers (the §2.3.2 block for counter 1
# and the §2.4.2 encryption of the RFC's own 114-byte plaintext, hex for hex, with decrypt round-trip), and Zig's own std.crypto
# ChaCha20IETF as an independent implementation, byte-for-byte over the empty message, a 5-byte input, an exact-64-byte boundary, and a
# 200-byte multi-block input at a raised counter. The vendored Monocypher source is a held network fetch, so Monocypher-source parity is
# an honest horizon rung. A length-mismatched or over-long call refuses before a byte is enciphered. Purely local arithmetic — the key
# and nonce are the RFC's public vectors, no real secret, no signature, no network, no funds, no real device.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/crypto_chacha20_witness.rish

say "crypto-chacha20: Language EN — Season G's third primitive: ChaCha20 (IETF, RFC 8439), Monocypher's stream cipher and the keystream the ChaCha/Poly line is built on, the direct next stone after the two hashes toward the Rye-native encryption and signing stack. Pure 32-bit integer arithmetic, no big-integer field math, so it is genuinely buildable in authored Rye this round, and it carries the published RFC 8439 known-answers that tie parity to a neutral public reference."
say "crypto-chacha20: Style Radiant — the law is stated positively: a sixteen-word state (four constant words, eight key words, one block counter, three nonce words) folds through twenty rounds of the quarter-round — four adds, four XORs, four fixed left-rotations (16, 12, 8, 7) — adds the initial state back, and serializes little-endian into a 64-byte keystream block the message is XORed against, the counter rising per block, so the same key, counter, and nonce always yield the same keystream anyone can replay against the RFC."
say "crypto-chacha20: Lens TAME — the primitive calls no std.crypto; it is authored from the RFC, bounded input (max_message_bytes), contract asserts on every function, wrapping +% where the math is mod 2^32. Parity holds against the RFC 8439 known-answers AND Zig's independent std.crypto ChaCha20IETF byte-for-byte over the empty message, a 5-byte input, a 64-byte-boundary input, and a multi-block input at a raised counter; a length-mismatched or over-long call refuses before a byte is enciphered. Monocypher-source parity is a named horizon behind the held vendor fetch. Purely local — the key and nonce are the RFC's public vectors, no real secret, no signature, no network, no funds, no device."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "crypto/bin/chacha20"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p crypto/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build crypto/chacha20.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "crypto-chacha20: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "crypto/bin/chacha20.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "crypto-chacha20: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "crypto-chacha20: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN crypto-chacha20' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "crypto-chacha20: selftest did not print the GREEN crypto-chacha20 line"
